  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A coastal low will pass by or through the \nregion Sunday into Monday.\n\nHigh pressure over the Mid-Atlantic will begin shifting off the\ncoast of New England leaving one more tranquil day today with\nabove normal temperatures before showers return to the region. \nWith the on Saturday. \n\nMeanwhile, broad troughing continues to reside over the \nSoutheast United States. As some upper energy arrives within the\nsub-tropical jet today, a closed surface low will develop and \nstrengthen to the east of Florida over the weekend.\n\nWhile the 12Z suite of guidance continued to struggle, the 00z\nsuite and the ensembles, both dynamical and AI, are continuing a\neastern trend in the development of the surface low track by\nSunday afternoon. This trend in the eastern shift continues to\npush the rain totals slightly lower with widespread rain\ndeveloping late Sunday morning and then persisting into Monday\nmorning. The Weather Prediction Center continues to highlight a\nmarginal risk for excessive rainfall with the system. \n\nThe National Hurricane Center is continuing to reduce the\nlikelihood of tropical cyclone formation down to 20% with the\novernight updates of the tropical weather outlook. Where the low\ntakes on a warm core or not, periods of heavy rain, modestly \ngusty winds (mainly for coastal areas), and coastal flooding may\noccur. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Another low pressure system may approach the \narea around Thanksgiving.\n\nAfter the coastal low departs our region on Monday, a strong cold \nfront will pass through the region with high pressure building in \nits wake. Below normal temperatures are expected Tuesday and \nWednesday. \n\nGuidance has been very consistent for this range that a strong upper-\nlow will move eastward out of the South Central US into portions of \nthe Southeast on Wednesday. Downstream of the upper-low, conditions \nwill become increasingly favorable for additional surface \ncyclogenesis off the Southeast of southern Mid-Atlantic coasts. \nCurrent guidance suggests that whatever low forms may approach the \nregion on or around Thanksgiving. Guidance is consistent enough that \nthe formation of an additional low pressure system is high \nprobability. However, at this range, it remains too soon to get into \nany potential specific impacts to the region. \n\nFor now, the Thanksgiving time period certainly bears watching with \nunsettled weather a distinct possibility.",
